I want to welcome everyone to the 10th  annual Gail any a BPD conference we're  so appreciative to have you here today  we're so appreciative that the  conference has been going on for 10  years I'm going to let Perry Hoffman  speak a little bit to the history of the  conference that she and Seth Axelrod  started together but I think what this  has done for a lot of us has offer an  opportunity for many different people  involved in the treatment process for  BPD to come together and listen to each  other and listen to each other's  experiences and learn from one another  and so I hope by you being here today  that you'll also have that experience  and that we can learn from you and you  can learn from us see I want to start  off today hopefully by thanking the  providers the sponsors of the conference  Yale has provided this wonderful  location for us but they've also  provided a lot of resources okay there  we go so we want to thank any a BPD for  all of their support and for the mission  that they've dedicated to educating and  helping individuals with borderline  personality disorder to the Yale New  Haven psychiatric hospital who's been a  wonderful sponsor through the years to  the Yale stress Center where a treatment  and research come together the Yale  School of Medicine Department of  Psychiatry where this conference is now  based and the support that they've  provided i also want to thank our  co-sponsors clearview treatment and  silver hill and i also want to thank the  many people that have volunteered an  incredible amount of time to bring this  conference together for today the  conference coordinators Suzanne Decker  Francesca Jackie Smith Trish Woodward  our conference staff I also want to  acknowledge you'll notice  at the to the right of the stage that we  have interpreting services both terry  mcauliffe and rachel donahue are from  the Department of rehab services and  they are being provided to us today by  the Yale New Haven hospitals  interpreting services department the  deaf and hard of hearing services so we  thank them very much for the work that  they're doing you'll also see today at  registration and throughout the day that  there have been a number of volunteers  that are listed here that have provided  countless hours to help us get this  conference together so we thank them all  heartily thank you thank you Emily and  thank you Emily and her team who've  really and done an incredible job  running these conferences is a full-time  job and when it's not your full-time  paid job it really is quite a feat to  pull off and I know there are over 300  people coming here today so thank you  for such a fabulous job so as Emily said  this is the 10th year of this conference  and I just want to give you a little  history about it we any a BPD Andy and  I'll tell you a little bit about who we  are in a minute but we started doing our  first conference in 2002 we did it at  Columbia University we had over 450  people there over two days and it was  the first national conference on  borderline personality disorder well  Seth Axelrod who is the person who  usually is the chairperson at this  conference and a co-director stalked me  at the next day ABT convention i think  it was in New Orleans or something and i  truly mean he stalked me until he found  me and he said we want to do one of  these conferences at Yale he said we'll  help you do it whatever you need we'll  make it happen and that was the  beginning of what's now a 10-year  partnership so we're sorry that Seth  can't be here we know he's recovering  and we're very grateful for that and I  just wanted to show you the conference's  that we have done over the past decade  so you can just run down to that we  started in 2005 we talked about very  basic stuff then we went on to current  research and treatment went on to  high-risk adolescents and their families  next was trauma and resiliency  and 2009 problems with substance abuse  210 obesity and eating disorders 211  common and distinguishing aspects of dbt  and MBT 12 was impulsivity aggression  and legal involvement 30 20 13 was  managing related cognitive challenges  and bring us bringing us to today with a  decade of development so we hope we and  we invite you to go on our website where  you can download for free all of these  conferences on video and at your leisure  review what's happened over the past  decade so I just also want to tell you a  little bit about our organization our  name is the national education alliance  for borderline personality disorder we  were started in August twentieth two  thousand and one about three weeks  before 911 when we think back how we  were all so innocent that day working on  the disorder and then the world changed  after three weeks after that but there  were seven of us in the room and we were  family members consumers and one mental  health professional and we spent about  10 hours in that room was actually at  New York Hospital in my office I was  working there then was on a Saturday and  we decided that we needed to do  something that this disorder was not  being recognized by the National  Alliance on Mental Illness and within 10  hours we came up with our name and we  came up with an event to do and that was  the first conference that i just  mentioned that we did at columbia in  2001 we are an all-volunteer  organization those of you that see  people speaking today you'll see the  Costello's later this afternoon none of  us get paid for what we do we do it for  the passion we have for the disorder and  for the improvement of lives of people  impacted by BPD there's a dbt listserv  those of you that are DBT therapists  know that yesterday a question came  across the listserv what is the ladybug  about in for awareness and the ladybug  was because each one of the seven of us  had gone at some points in the hospital  either to the cafeteria to the ladies  room and we each came back and said oh I  saw a ladybug we each saw a lady bug in  a different room in the  hospital so the ladybug was born that  day with us and has stayed with us  forever we tried to take it out of our  logo once and we got such an outcry of  emails that the ladybug is is permanent  now I just want to tell you a little bit  about what we do and the reason I'm  telling you about this is that we are a  resource resource for all of you whether  you're a family member or person in  recovery or professional please do go to  our website and use what's there  everything on that site is for free so  we've hosted about 60 conferences we  have a call in series which is really  incredible it's on Sunday nights it's  from eight to nine p.m. it's each week a  different researcher or clinician we've  had the good fortune of having Charlie  Swenson and Kelly Kerner once a month  doing connecting the dots people sign up  to be on the call just because we need  to know how many people will be there we  send out handouts the week before all of  those call ins are also on our website  it's probably the richest callin series  that you can find on the disorder so we  invite you to use it if you'd like to  sign up to be on the call go to our  website and the directions are there we  also on our website have over 150 audio  and video recordings with our  conferences and Collins we have an  online course that's there it was  designed by Louis joy Kane who you will  hear this afternoon John Gunderson and  brian palmer it's on our site for free  it's on Harvard's medical college site  for cost because you can get CME credits  on it we've done two books one done by  the American Psychiatric Association the  other is a book it's the only social  work textbook on borderline personality  disorder and we've just now saw it  recently signed a contract with new  harbinger press with dr. John Gunderson  where we're going to be publishing an  anthology of stories of recovery we hear  so much about people struggling with the  disorder that we felt it was also time  to share the stories of people who have  who serve as inspiration for other  people the the thing that I'm going to  talk about now for a minute we're  exceptionally excited about it's a think  tank that we hosted last week  at the APA they were 25 of the leading  people who are involved in early  detection on borderline personality  disorder those of you who are in the  field and maybe or those of you who have  relatives with the BP with BPD have been  told that you can't diagnose someone  under 18 and in fact actually the  opposite is true you really need to  diagnose someone under 18 because that's  the way people are going to get better  so there were 25 of us we did a think  tank we stayed in one room for six hours  and came up with some action plans to  make sure that this now be is front and  center for the disorder and not  something that people will not talk  about and lastly is our hallmark which  is our family connections program and  you'll hear much more about that and  it'll come alive to you by to wonderful  leaders the Costello's so thank you for  coming think about Mei Mei as BPD  awareness month they are we have our  wristbands we asked for suggested  donation of five dollars it helps  support what we do when we hope that  you'll wear one throughout the month of  May thank you I'd like to ask dr.  William sledge to join me up here dr.  sledge is the George and Esther gross  professor of psychiatry he's also the  deputy chair for clinical affairs and  he's also the medical director for the  Yale New Haven psychiatric hospital so  dr. sledge please join us on stage Thank  You Emily and thank you dr. Hoffman for  your contributions that you and South  have brought to us and colleagues ladies  and gentlemen all of you those of you  who are family members those of you who  suffers of this illness we welcome you  to this venue we're very proud to be a  part of it I think I may have introduced  the first one maybe the second one I'm  not sure which and but this program has  grown up under our species and we're  extremely proud to be here today it's  good to have such a robust audience of  people this condition needs a robust  audience we  advocates providers people who are  intimately familiar with the  circumstances and who are willing to put  in the time and the effort to learn how  to treat it effectively we're all  intimate acquaintances of borderline  personality disorder and we're all in it  together let me begin by thanking the  sponsors again just to make clear that  we are deeply indebted to them the only  Haven Hospital and the system of Yale  New Haven the Department of Psychiatry  of the medical school or have been with  this sense of beginning of course the  national educational Alliance borderline  personality disorder this is a true  partnership with with you and the yelled  stress center and the clearview  treatment center and of course silver  Hill Hospital all to places that join us  in treating this condition and a special  thanks to Emily fulfilling in for Seth  this is much appreciated for those of  you who may not know emily is a gifted  psychotherapy researcher and it's my  dream that someday we'll be able to  figure out a way that she can work it  yale-new Haven psychiatric hospital I  want to acknowledge you dr. Hoffman for  your steadfast partnership in this  endeavor we wouldn't be here without you  and your work with Seth on this project  has been a true act of love and  effective love over these decade and we  continue and hope it will continue for  past all of our times here as long as  it's needed and I want to make that Seth  would be here today I realized he's not  and but I know he's going to see this  and so I want to make sure that he  understands that we're thinking of him  that we appreciate his leadership and  his part in this in the past and in the  future and his persistence in creating  this program in this opportunity we look  forward to having  you back Seth and take care of yourself  and your recovery and we'll see you soon  you're the man  so I think separate from Seth and Perry  is Seth and Perry together in a sense  it's where it's one of those things  where the sum is more than the elements  individually and so I just want to  acknowledge the importance of your  partnership and how you've worked so  well together over this all this time  and how we've been able to continue to  have this to be such an effective  program and so for that we're deeply in  your debt to both of you the time is  right for us to consider and reflect on  the history and the condition of  borderline personality in its connection  to suicidality and other distress states  this field has been a leadership also in  the development of effective  psychotherapies in a general sort of way  for whatever reason the ability to  conceptualize study implement and  reflect on different approaches has been  a premier aspect of the field of the  development of psychological treatments  at least for borderline personality the  rest of the areas of Psychiatry can look  to you for leadership and how to do this  we have a great body of evidence that  supports the effectiveness of  psychological approaches to borderline  personality and we all need to further  that evidence to develop it to  disseminate it to practice it and to  make sure that we practice it well we  have multiple treatments as I mentioned  with different approaches and I'll have  some evidence of effectiveness some  great evidence the presence of these  different approaches though representing  fundamental differences about how we  view human nature have a common pathway  to success and provide us with a  fleeting glimpse of what are the  effective elements of psychotherapy if  we are careful in how we study them and  compare them and think of them so let me  challenge you all today to keep that in  mind as you hear these different  approaches and as you think about them  and you think about what roles they may  play in your operations in your life  these side by side comparisons make us  wiser and we have to be open to  different approaches particularly those  that we're not familiar with so you all  of you here have signaled your interest  in this task by virtue of you being here  and I challenge you to take it up with  energy and gusto and make it easier for  people like me who manage or try to  manage the system to do a better job so  with that let me thank you for your  advocacy for your presence and let's let  the show begin Emily we get started with  with Suzanne Witter Holt there's a  couple more things I want to say I  realized as everyone was talking that  there there are people that usually are  present here that are not that have not  been spoken about yet but the first  person that is actually here that I want  to mention who's also been a key part of  the Yale any a BPD conference through  the years it's Trish Woodward who has  played such an integral role through the  years and helping us plan this  conference and volunteered so much of  her time so so I wanted to just say  thank you Trish for all your work for  through the years for that  she in fact might still be out at the  front table Hershey upstair yeah there  you are thank you Trish and the other  thing that has been briefly mentioned I  just want to clarify so as you might  notice if you've been at this conference  before I am not usually the face of this  conference up here usually Seth Axelrod  is the person moderating for the day and  as you've heard mentioned he's not here  he is on medical leave right now but I  did just speak to him this morning and  he sends his well wishes and wants to  thank you all for coming and he  absolutely plans on being here next year  playing this role so he looks forward to  seeing all of you back next year
 

